{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiToxXJCsRBesGkw8Q3t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the candidate with a different mechanism, and it passes the self-check (`OK`, smoke-fit ELPD-LOO −2316.0). The novelty gate wasn't tested, since the self-check doesn't cover it.\n\n**Why the first attempt was rejected:** its `candidate.py` was the same code as the live model `personal_chance_switch_belief`; only the comments differed. That's why its predictions matched exactly (RMSE 0.00000).\n\n**New model: `person_side_bias_chance_vs_motif`**, a refinement of the incumbent `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`.\n- **What stays:** a sequence still looks random when an over-alternating fair coin explains it better than the regular generators (switch-biased Markov coin, biased coin, repeating motif with slips). The evidence is still weighed per flip, and each person still has their own decisiveness.\n- **The one change:** each person also has their own habitual lean towards the left or right button, drawn from a population centred near zero. It is added to the evidence before the choice.\n- **Why:** it targets the critique's second discrepancy. People's rates of choosing the left sequence vary more than the incumbent predicts (p = 0.016, q = 0.064).\n\nI didn't target the stronger discrepancy, about people who prefer fewer switches (q = 0.016). Two live models already address it, `personal_chance_switch_belief` and `person_specific_chance_switch_belief`, and copying one of them is what got the first attempt rejected.\n\n**Risks at admission:**\n- **Novelty:** on the novelty pool the predictions are averaged over participants, and a side bias centred near zero mostly cancels out in that average. What remains should be a pull towards 0.5 on clear-cut pairs plus refitted parameters, which I expect but haven't checked to clear the 0.002 RMSE threshold. If it doesn't, this second rejection is final for the slot.\n- **Fit:** the smoke fit flagged 4.7% of trials with high Pareto k, so its PSIS-LOO counts as unreliable. That's likely from the short single-chain fit and 400 extra person-level parameters, but the full fit could show the same. It took about 3.5 minutes for 200 iterations, so the full fit should stay well under the 30-minute limit, though that is an estimate.\n\nFiles are in `.../experiment2/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_4_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62405,"cache_read_input_tokens":10102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":62405,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bca468da-5671-4115-923c-a2121ab87f7a","uuid":"dc5bbf11-2408-4017-ab42-163c8a28d1a9","timestamp":"2026-10-05T04:31:31.606Z","request_id":"req_011CfiTowcDdAWG6hJydVFH3"}
